To make ESLint ignore all rules for a single line, you can use a comment to disable and re-enable the rules. Here’s how you can do it:

1. **Disable all rules for the line:**
   Add a comment at the beginning of the line to disable all rules. You can use the `/*eslint-disable */` comment.

2. **Re-enable all rules for the next line:**
   Add a comment at the beginning of the next line to re-enable all rules. You can use the `/*eslint-enable */` comment.

Here is an example:

```javascript
// Disable all rules for this line
/*eslint-disable */
console.log('This line will not follow any ESLint rules.');
// Re-enable all rules for the next line
/*eslint-enable */
```

Alternatively, you can disable specific rules for the line if you want to keep other rules enabled. For example, if you want to disable the `no-console` rule, you can use:

```javascript
// Disable no-console rule for this line
/*eslint no-console: "off" */
console.log('This line will not follow the no-console rule.');
// Re-enable no-console rule for the next line
/*eslint no-console: "on" */
```

If you want to ignore all rules for the entire file, you can use the `/*eslint-disable-file */` comment at the top of the file. However, this is not recommended as it can make it harder to maintain the codebase.

```javascript
// Disable all rules for the entire file
/*eslint-disable-file */
```

Remember that disabling rules can make your code harder to maintain and can lead to issues if not used judiciously. It's generally better to fix the issues that ESLint is pointing out.

